<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taprobane</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taprobane was the ancient Greek and Roman name for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, derived from Tambapanni, the name of the first Sinhalese settlement. Greek writers learned of the island after the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, and it was described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> and Ptolemy, whose map greatly exaggerated its size. The name stayed in use in European geography until the early modern period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taprobane.jpg" alt="Taprobane" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ptolemy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptolemy%27s_Map_of_Taprobane.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
